# faceauth PAM example (installed as /usr/share/doc/faceauth/pam-example).
#
# Put the faceauth line FIRST in the "auth" section of the service you want,
# e.g. /etc/pam.d/sudo, /etc/pam.d/polkit-1, your screen locker, or system-auth:
#
#   auth  sufficient  pam_exec.so quiet /usr/bin/faceauth-auth
#
# "sufficient": a recognized face (exit 0) finishes authentication; any other
# exit code is ignored and PAM continues with the next line (your password).
# Keep a working password line below it — face auth never replaces it.
#
# User name: pam_exec passes PAM_USER in the environment. Do NOT write
# "-u $USER": PAM does not expand shell variables, the literal "$USER" would be
# used. Use "-u <name>" only to pin a single-user machine to one account.
#
# faceauth-auth exit codes:
#   0   face matched
#   10  skipped: `faceauth disable`, remote session (SSH / PAM_RHOST),
#       lid closed, or no model enrolled for the user
#   11  no match before the timeout ([video] timeout)
#   12  setup error: config, camera, models, or an untrusted model file
#   13  every frame was too dark (IR emitter off? see linux-enable-ir-emitter)
#
# Remote sessions are skipped on purpose ([auth] skip_remote = true): otherwise
# whoever sits in front of the laptop could approve a `sudo` typed over SSH.
#
# Check the setup with:  sudo faceauth doctor
# Logs:                   /var/log/faceauth.log  (FACEAUTH_LOG=debug for more)
